WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Suppose T of X has odd integer
coefficients on a cycle of length two to

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:09.240
the k. Its product with T star is two
to the power two t, times w squared,

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.620
where t is at least two and w
is odd. We will halve the cycle,

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.040
keep every coefficient odd, and
divide the scalar norm by four.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:24.160
The parameters will change from
k, t to k minus one, t minus one.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:30.260
The comparison lemma says that opposite
coefficients agree modulo two to the t.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.670
Since t is at least two,
they agree modulo four.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:40.360
Write one opposite pair as a sub
j and a sub j plus four r sub j.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:44.180
Their average is a sub j plus two r sub j:

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.260
an odd integer plus an even
integer, hence still odd.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.100
For example, three and
seven average to five.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.400
Apply this averaging
to every opposite pair.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.000
The old cycle folds onto a
cycle of half the length,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.520
with one odd average at each new position.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.780
Now check the norm. Let q reduce
exponents modulo the half length,

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.047
and call the polynomial of averages T one.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.820
Opposite coefficients
add under projection,

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.160
so q of T equals twice T one.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.380
The projection preserves
multiplication and star.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.320
Applying it to the original
norm identity therefore gives

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.940
four times T one times T one

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:37.120
star equals two to the power
two t, times w squared.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.320
Divide the integer coefficients by four.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.820
This is valid because the integer group
ring has a free coefficient basis.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.820
The new norm is two raised to twice
the difference between t and one,

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:56.700
times w squared. One fold has halved
the length and divided the norm by four.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.200
Keep the indices visible as we repeat.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.470
Start with k equal to two s and t
equal to s, assuming s is at least two.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.420
After j folds, k equals two s minus j,

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.100
and t equals s minus j. The initial row,

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:20.845
j equal to zero, is the projection
from Node two point three.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.700
Whenever j is less than s minus
one, t is still at least two,

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.660
so the next fold is allowed and
every coefficient stays odd.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:33.700
After exactly s minus one folds,

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.380
k equals s plus one and t equals one.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:43.300
The length is two to the power s plus
one, and the norm is four w squared.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:49.340
Stop here. We do not apply another
oddness preserving fold at t equal to one.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.800
The next node uses this endpoint
to obtain the contradiction.
